\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces General-instruction 8B comparison. IF, TF, Hon and Help are independent local-judge win rates against the common base response under four attribute rubrics, averaged over both presentation orders with ties counted $0.5$; $\min _k\Delta _k$ is the smallest of the four. The first three rows are measured at one seed on the frozen $2{,}000$-prompt final split, $16{,}000$ judgments per arm, with the base row equal to $0.5$ by definition rather than judged. $N_{\rm  eval}$ is the number of prompts whose judgments parsed in both orders. Only helpfulness moves decisively, $+.058$ for NBPO and $+.048$ for the utilitarian control; honesty sits at parity for both. The two arms are not separated by this evaluation, which is what a target total variation of $.0195$ predicts. Remaining rows and the seed expansion are unmeasured. The DPO row uses uniform weights; the figure retains every scalarization setting.}}{7}{table.62}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:uf-objectives}{{1}{7}{General-instruction 8B comparison. IF, TF, Hon and Help are independent local-judge win rates against the common base response under four attribute rubrics, averaged over both presentation orders with ties counted $0.5$; $\min _k\Delta _k$ is the smallest of the four. The first three rows are measured at one seed on the frozen $2{,}000$-prompt final split, $16{,}000$ judgments per arm, with the base row equal to $0.5$ by definition rather than judged. $N_{\rm eval}$ is the number of prompts whose judgments parsed in both orders. Only helpfulness moves decisively, $+.058$ for NBPO and $+.048$ for the utilitarian control; honesty sits at parity for both. The two arms are not separated by this evaluation, which is what a target total variation of $.0195$ predicts. Remaining rows and the seed expansion are unmeasured. The DPO row uses uniform weights; the figure retains every scalarization setting}{table.62}{}}
