\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Within-objective cyclicity, measured. Rates use all $100$ planned prompts and $n$ counts tournaments complete in both panels; requiring all ten scheduled judgments per edge leaves $2$--$19$ prompts unresolved per rubric, and those are not read as cycle-free. Excess is in percentage points with a $95\%$ prompt-bootstrap interval from $2000$ replicates, against $2000$ fitted-null simulations. Observed repeated cycles $R$ are $0$--$1$ against a fitted sampling-noise expectation $R_{\mathrm  {BT}}$ of $0.24$--$0.97$, so no rubric shows an excess and every Holm-adjusted $p$ is $1.00$. $C_A$ counts prompts with any cycle in panel A, including length-four cycles: $9$, $2$, $5$, $4$ and $1$ over the four rubrics and the control, from $13$, $2$, $5$, $4$ and $1$ cyclic triangles against $400$ planned. This does not establish that the judge's preferences are transitive; it establishes that this budget found no cyclicity separable from sampling noise.}}{23}{table.139}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:within-rubric-cycles}{{9}{23}{Within-objective cyclicity, measured. Rates use all $100$ planned prompts and $n$ counts tournaments complete in both panels; requiring all ten scheduled judgments per edge leaves $2$--$19$ prompts unresolved per rubric, and those are not read as cycle-free. Excess is in percentage points with a $95\%$ prompt-bootstrap interval from $2000$ replicates, against $2000$ fitted-null simulations. Observed repeated cycles $R$ are $0$--$1$ against a fitted sampling-noise expectation $R_{\mathrm {BT}}$ of $0.24$--$0.97$, so no rubric shows an excess and every Holm-adjusted $p$ is $1.00$. $C_A$ counts prompts with any cycle in panel A, including length-four cycles: $9$, $2$, $5$, $4$ and $1$ over the four rubrics and the control, from $13$, $2$, $5$, $4$ and $1$ cyclic triangles against $400$ planned. This does not establish that the judge's preferences are transitive; it establishes that this budget found no cyclicity separable from sampling noise}{table.139}{}}
