\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Realized UF-4 pipeline counts, measured from the campaign artifacts. Every value is a measurement, not a target. The $20.00\%$ generation cap rate and the zero teacher-encoding truncation have different denominators and measure different problems: the first counts responses that stopped at the $1024$-token horizon, the second counts serialized scoring sequences that exceeded the encoder budget. Reference-tensor antisymmetry is imposed by construction and is an invariant, not evidence of correctness. The final block reports development diagnostics of the neural projection, which measure how well the fitted log-ratio tracks the frozen target and are not generated-policy outcomes; no independent evaluation of generated responses is in this table. For scale, the SafeRLHF panel's three seeds reached nMSE $.6845\pm .0048$ and sign accuracy $.7009\pm .0016$ against a target RMS of $1.125$, so the two panels' nMSE are comparable at very different target scales and neither is a policy result. The Nash and utilitarian arms realize their own targets almost identically, which is what a target total variation of $.0195$ predicts and is not a policy comparison. The audit-pilot block reports only parser and throughput measurements on the $20$ pilot prompts, which are excluded from every audit estimate; the strong first-position skew in its raw verdicts is why the protocol evaluates both presentation orders and reports the order-averaged estimand. The capability block compares the two trained checkpoints with the base under one fixed harness recipe; every difference there is smaller than the harness standard error, which is a statement about retention and not about the four objectives.}}{30}{table.156}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:uf4-realized}{{14}{30}{Realized UF-4 pipeline counts, measured from the campaign artifacts. Every value is a measurement, not a target. The $20.00\%$ generation cap rate and the zero teacher-encoding truncation have different denominators and measure different problems: the first counts responses that stopped at the $1024$-token horizon, the second counts serialized scoring sequences that exceeded the encoder budget. Reference-tensor antisymmetry is imposed by construction and is an invariant, not evidence of correctness. The final block reports development diagnostics of the neural projection, which measure how well the fitted log-ratio tracks the frozen target and are not generated-policy outcomes; no independent evaluation of generated responses is in this table. For scale, the SafeRLHF panel's three seeds reached nMSE $.6845\pm .0048$ and sign accuracy $.7009\pm .0016$ against a target RMS of $1.125$, so the two panels' nMSE are comparable at very different target scales and neither is a policy result. The Nash and utilitarian arms realize their own targets almost identically, which is what a target total variation of $.0195$ predicts and is not a policy comparison. The audit-pilot block reports only parser and throughput measurements on the $20$ pilot prompts, which are excluded from every audit estimate; the strong first-position skew in its raw verdicts is why the protocol evaluates both presentation orders and reports the order-averaged estimand. The capability block compares the two trained checkpoints with the base under one fixed harness recipe; every difference there is smaller than the harness standard error, which is a statement about retention and not about the four objectives}{table.156}{}}
