\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Nash versus mass-matched utilitarian target diagnostics, labelled by panel. TV and relative pairwise-target RMS measure different quantities. The UF-4 rows are recomputed from the stored arrays independently of the solver: the utilitarian solution attains higher unregularized $F$ but pays more proximal KL, so $J$ still favours Nash, and matching the coefficient $L_1$ therefore does not match realized movement. The SafeRLHF rows retain their earlier reported provenance.}}{33}{table.179}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:aggregation-targets}{{21}{33}{Nash versus mass-matched utilitarian target diagnostics, labelled by panel. TV and relative pairwise-target RMS measure different quantities. The UF-4 rows are recomputed from the stored arrays independently of the solver: the utilitarian solution attains higher unregularized $F$ but pays more proximal KL, so $J$ still favours Nash, and matching the coefficient $L_1$ therefore does not match realized movement. The SafeRLHF rows retain their earlier reported provenance}{table.179}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Paired Nash minus utilitarian differences, conditional on each policy seed. The SafeRLHF rows are teacher-defined surplus differences on the same $1{,}000$ prompts, with their reported interval construction retained from the artifacts. The UF-4 rows are a different quantity: per-prompt differences of the independent judge's order-averaged win rate against the identical cached base response, over roughly $1{,}950$ prompts scored in both arms, with $2000$ whole-prompt bootstrap intervals. Pairing removes prompt difficulty and is tighter than comparing the two arms' separately reported win rates; all four UF-4 differences still contain zero and their signs are mixed. Intervals containing zero do not establish equivalence, and these measure prompt variability under one seed, not seed uncertainty.}}{33}{table.181}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:aggregation-policy-pairs}{{22}{33}{Paired Nash minus utilitarian differences, conditional on each policy seed. The SafeRLHF rows are teacher-defined surplus differences on the same $1{,}000$ prompts, with their reported interval construction retained from the artifacts. The UF-4 rows are a different quantity: per-prompt differences of the independent judge's order-averaged win rate against the identical cached base response, over roughly $1{,}950$ prompts scored in both arms, with $2000$ whole-prompt bootstrap intervals. Pairing removes prompt difficulty and is tighter than comparing the two arms' separately reported win rates; all four UF-4 differences still contain zero and their signs are mixed. Intervals containing zero do not establish equivalence, and these measure prompt variability under one seed, not seed uncertainty}{table.181}{}}
